  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nGusty northwest winds (2530 mph) behind a cold front will \ncontinue ushering in a cooler, more seasonal airmass tonight. \nTemperatures will drop into the 30 and low 40s this evening, \nwith overnight lows reaching the upper 20s to low 30s. \nConditions will remain chilly tomorrow as breezy northwest winds\npersist ahead of a surface high moving into the Upper Midwest. \nAfternoon highs will reach the lower 40s under partly cloudy \nskies. \n\nThe aforementioned surface high will settle in over the \nwestern Great Lakes Monday night into Tuesday. Though this will \nturn low-level flow north-northeasterly off the lake, lake \neffect parameters will remain poor, owing to the presence of a \nstout inversion based around 6,000 feet. Accordingly, we do not \nexpect more than a few light rain or snow showers near the lake \nearly Tuesday morning. Otherwise, expect another seasonably \nchilly day Tuesday, with high temperatures in the low to mid \n40s. \n\nBy midweek, an incredibly complex larger scale weather \npattern is expected to evolve across the CONUS. A trio of waves \nextending from the southern Canadian prairies to northeast \nMexico on Tuesday are progged to interact, in some form, across \nthe eastern CONUS late Wednesday through Thanksgiving, setting \nthe stage for active weather over a potentially large corridor \neast of the Mississippi River. Unsurprisingly, the ensemble \nspread of solutions remains quite expansive, with solutions \nranging from little to no notable weather over our area to wet \nsnow for western portions of the forecast area. The later \nsolution would be most impactful, with an eventual congealment \nof the three waves occurring around the Upper Ohio River Valley \nas dynamic cooling increases chances for mixed precip/snow. Will\nneed to closely monitor this period, but the unique interaction\nbetween each of the waves begets great uncertainty for at least\na couple more days.\n\nKJB/Kluber",
